From a8b2ecc67f7cc04a7f5ba9cc4399184786bdeca9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Joachim Wiberg <troglobit@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 11:20:16 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] net: sparx5: apply the priority map of the root qdisc in the
 fabric
Organization: Wires

An ets qdisc is offloaded only with the plain reversed priomap, so a
root qdisc that queues priority 1 below priority 0, as IEEE 802.1Q
Table 8-5 does, is refused:

    tc qdisc add dev eth0 root ets bands 8 strict 8 priomap 6 7 5 4 3 2 1 0

An mqprio or taprio map is taken, yet holds for the CPU path only.
Frames forwarded by the fabric are queued by the priority their ingress
port classified, whatever the map says.

The fabric has no table between priority and queue.  The QoS class a
port assigns at ingress is the queue on every port the frame leaves by,
and the rewriter maps that class back to PCP and DSCP.

Keep the map of the root qdisc per port.  Compose it into the PCP, DSCP
and default priority classification tables, and its inverse into the
rewrite tables, so a frame is queued as the map says and leaves with the
PCP and DSCP of its priority.  Where several priorities share a class,
the lowest of them is written back.  Frames the CPU receives get their
priority from the classified QoS class in the extraction header, and
frames it sends carry their class in the injection header, as lan966x
and the Ocelot tagger do.

Signed-off-by: Joachim Wiberg <troglobit@gmail.com>
